{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "weno3_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc",
    "description": "Hybrid WENO3/ENO1 gradient magnitude |grad u| = sqrt(u_x^2 + u_y^2) for the level-set Hamilton-Jacobi term psi_t = -S|grad psi| on cartesian_uniform_2d with the linear-extrapolation halo closure -- NCAR fire_behavior's fire_upwinding = 8 (physics/level_set_mod.F90 Calc_tend_ls case 8; Munoz-Esparza et al. 2018): grad = sqrt(diff2x^2 + diff2y^2) with diff2x, diff2y the per-axis hybrid upwind derivatives of rules/weno3_eno1_D1x_extrapolate_bc and rules/weno3_eno1_D1y_extrapolate_bc. It is the third-order sibling of rules/weno5_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc (fire_upwinding = 9) and differs from it in exactly one respect: the in-band reconstruction is the two-stencil classical Jiang-Shu WENO3 of stencils/weno3_js_face_recon instead of the three-stencil WENO5 of stencils/weno5_js_face_recon. The band test |u[i,j]| < weno_band_cells*dx, the ENO1 wall band, the fourth-order wind-relative upwind selector and the wall closure are byte-for-byte what option 9 uses, because case (8) and case (9) of the reference differ only in which Select_weno* they call. NOT REUSABLE FROM ELSEWHERE: the only other WENO3 in this library is grids/cartesian_uniform_1d/stencils/weno3_recon, which is WENO-Z (Borges 2008; weights gamma_k (1 + (tau3/(eps+beta_k))^2) with tau3 = |beta0 - beta1|), a different nonlinear weighting from the classical gamma_k/(beta_k + eps)^2 the reference code computes; the two agree only where the field is smooth. COMPOUND MATCH (esm-spec 9.6.3): the whole compound rooted at the outer sqrt at priority 20 -- above the per-axis rules' 10 -- so it fires first when a document imports both; scoped with where {u: {shape: [x, y]}}. The body is sqrt(Mx^2 + My^2) where Mx and My are the two per-axis rules' makearrays verbatim (seven regions each: the hybrid interior block, the ENO1 strips, the two ghost wall lines); it is therefore not itself a makearray-rooted body and the tiling lint (L006) does not apply to it -- the two constituent makearrays are lint-tiled in their own rule files. A model must import this norm rule OR one of its siblings (weno5_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc, weno5_eno1_blend_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc, weno5_eno1_signed_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc), never two: they match the same sqrt-of-sum-of-squares pattern at the same priority 20. THE FRONT-DISTANCE SWITCH means a level set that is a signed distance function near its zero set is required for the band to mean 'near the front' (fire_behavior reinitialises for this reason). FREE NAMES: wind_u, wind_v ([x, y] fields), bdy_eno1_cells (>= 2), weno_band_cells, and the grid's dx, dy. Requires NX, NY >= 5. ORDER: formally first; third in the smooth WENO band. EXACT on a linear field (norm sqrt(a^2 + b^2)). BOUNDARY CLOSURE bc:extrapolate_linear: the walls carry no prescribed value; instead each wall cell sees a one-cell halo ghost extrapolated from the two innermost cells, u[0,j] = max(2 u[1,j] - u[2,j], u[1,j], u[2,j]) (and symmetrically u[NX+1,j], u[i,0], u[i,NY+1]) -- stencils/extrapolate_linear_ghost, fire_behavior's Extrapol_var_at_bdys (HALO = 1, BIAS = 1). Only the ENO1 (three-point) stencil is ever evaluated at a wall cell, so one ghost suffices, exactly as in the reference code, whose halo is one cell for the same reason. Corner cells use the two edge ghosts of their walls (the reference code's diagonal corner ghost is never read by ENO1).",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:rule",
      "family:finite_difference",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d",
      "op:grad_norm",
      "axes:x,y",
      "order:1",
      "stencil_width:5",
      "bc:extrapolate_linear",
      "spec:9.6.8"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Munoz-Esparza, D., Kosovic, B., Jimenez, P. A., & Coen, J. L. (2018). An accurate fire-spread algorithm in the Weather Research and Forecasting model using the level-set method. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(4), 908-926. The hybrid WENO/ENO1 level-set schemes (fire_upwinding = 8 and 9) of WRF-Fire / NCAR fire_behavior.",
        "doi": "10.1002/2017MS001108"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jiang, G.-S., & Shu, C.-W. (1996). Efficient implementation of weighted ENO schemes. Journal of Computational Physics, 126(1), 202-228. The classical WENO smoothness indicators beta_k and nonlinear weights w_k = gamma_k/(beta_k + eps)^2.",
        "doi": "10.1006/jcph.1996.0130"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Harten, A., Engquist, B., Osher, S., & Chakravarthy, S. R. (1987). Uniformly high order accurate essentially non-oscillatory schemes, III. Journal of Computational Physics, 71(2), 231-303. ENO stencil selection by smallest divided difference (first-order form: the minmod limiter).",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(87)90031-3"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Osher, S., & Sethian, J. A. (1988). Fronts propagating with curvature-dependent speed: Algorithms based on Hamilton-Jacobi formulations. Journal of Computational Physics, 79(1), 12-49.",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(88)90002-2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR Community Fire Behavior Model (fire_behavior), develop @ eb77580, physics/level_set_mod.F90: Calc_tend_ls, Select_eno, Select_weno3, Select_4th, Select_godunov (case 4), Extrapol_var_at_bdys. The transcribed reference implementation."
      }
    ]
  },
